│ function likely assumes a successful response (status code 200)    │
│ and doesn’t account for errors.                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ Reasoning: The find_anchor function is currently extracting the    │
│ anchor text directly from the response.text without checking the   │
│ HTTP status code. If the server returns an error (e.g., 404, 500), │
│ the response.text will contain an error message, and the anchor    │
│ extraction will fail, leading to the "Anchor not found" report. We │
│ need to add a check for the status code and handle errors          │
│ appropriately, likely by returning None or raising an exception.   │
│                                                                    │
│ Here's the patch:                                                  │
│                                                                    │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃                         modification 1                         ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  [file]source/sphinx/commands/linkcheck.py[/file]                  │
│  [original]def find_anchor(response_text):\n    anchor =           │
│  re.search(r'href=(&lt;#.*?&gt;#)', response_text)\n    if         │
│  anchor:\n        return anchor.group(1)[6:-1]\n    else:\n        │
│  return None\n[/original]                                          │
│  [patched]def find_anchor(response_text):\n    response =          │
│  requests.Response(response_text)\n    if response.status_code !=  │
│  200:\n        return None\n    anchor =                           │
│  re.search(r'href=(&lt;#.*?&gt;#)', response_text)\n    if         │
│  anchor:\n        return anchor.group(1)[6:-1]\n    else:\n        │
│  return None\n[/patched]                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃                         modification 2                         ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  [file]source/sphinx/commands/linkcheck.py[/file]                  │
│  [original]if anchor:\n    return anchor.group(1)[6:-1]\nelse:\n   │
│  return None\n[/original]                                          │
│  [patched]if anchor:\n    return anchor.group(1)[6:-1]\nelse:\n    │
│  return None\n[/patched]                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ This patch adds a check for the HTTP status code within the        │
│ find_anchor function. If the status code is not 200 (success), the │
│ function immediately returns None, indicating that an anchor could │
│ not be found.  The second modification simply repeats the existing │
│ code, ensuring no unintended changes.                              │
